facts:
**Categorical** data, when comparing categories, suits a **column (bar) graph**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.8]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,8.6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(9.0,0);
\foreach \y in {2,4,6} {\draw (-0.2,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,0) {$0$};
\draw (0.6,0) rectangle (1.8,4);
\draw (2.2,0) rectangle (3.4,7);
\draw (3.8,0) rectangle (5.0,2);
\draw (5.4,0) rectangle (6.6,6);
\draw (7.0,0) rectangle (8.2,3);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.2,0) {$A$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (2.8,0) {$B$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.4,0) {$C$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (6.0,0) {$D$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (7.6,0) {$E$};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.1,3.5) {Frequency};
\node[below] at (4.4,-1.0) {Category};
\node at (4.4,8.3) {Column graph};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

**Parts of a whole** (each category as a share of a total) suit a **sector (pie) graph** or divided bar graph.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) circle (2);
\draw (0,0)--(90:2);
\draw (0,0)--(0:2);
\draw (0,0)--(260:2);
\draw (0,0)--(200:2);
\node at (45:1.2) {$A$};
\node at (310:1.2) {$B$};
\node at (145:1.2) {$C$};
\node at (230:1.2) {$D$};
\node at (0,2.6) {Sector graph};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

**Change over time** with continuous data suits a **line graph**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.8]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,7.6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(7.6,0);
\foreach \y in {2,4,6} {\draw (-0.2,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,0) {$0$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5,6} {\draw (\x,0.1)--(\x,-0.1);}
\draw[thick] (1,2)--(2,4.5)--(3,3)--(4,5.5)--(5,6.5)--(6,4);
\foreach \p in {(1,2),(2,4.5),(3,3),(4,5.5),(5,6.5),(6,4)} {\fill \p circle (2.5pt);}
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.1,3.5) {Value};
\node[below] at (3.5,-1.0) {Time};
\node at (3.5,7.3) {Line graph};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The **shape** of a numerical dataset (clusters, gaps, outliers) suits a **dot plot**, **stem-and-leaf plot** or **histogram**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.8]
\draw[->] (-0.6,0)--(9.4,0);
\foreach \x in {0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} {\draw (\x,0.1)--(\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \x/\c in {1/1,2/3,3/4,4/2,8/1} {\foreach \k in {1,...,\c} {\fill (\x,{0.35*\k}) circle (2.2pt);}}
\node[below] at (4,-0.9) {Score};
\node at (4,2.3) {Dot plot};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
A student wants to display their classmates’ favourite sports using a line graph.
Explain why a line graph is not appropriate for this data.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,9.4);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (6.6,0);
\foreach \y in {2,4,6,8} {\draw (-0.15,\y) -- (0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.15,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.15,0) {$0$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.2,0) {Soccer};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (2.6,0) {Netball};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.0,0) {Tennis};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (5.4,0) {Cricket};
\draw[thick] (1.2,8) -- (2.6,5) -- (4.0,7) -- (5.4,3);
\foreach \p in {(1.2,8),(2.6,5),(4.0,7),(5.4,3)} {\fill \p circle (2.5pt);}
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.1,4.0) {Number of students};
\node[below] at (3.3,-1.0) {Sport};
\node at (3.3,9.1) {Favourite sports};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

mastery.question_text:
The shoe sizes of a class are recorded (a numerical, discrete dataset) and you want to see the most common size and the spread.
A student chooses a line graph.
Explain why the line graph is a poor choice and name a better display.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.75]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,11.4);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (7.0,0);
\foreach \y in {2,4,6,8,10} {\draw (-0.15,\y) -- (0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.15,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.15,0) {$0$};
\foreach \x/\s in {1/5, 2/6, 3/7, 4/8, 5/9, 6/10} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\s$};}
\draw[thick] (1,2) -- (2,6) -- (3,9) -- (4,7) -- (5,4) -- (6,1);
\foreach \p in {(1,2),(2,6),(3,9),(4,7),(5,4),(6,1)} {\fill \p circle (2.5pt);}
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.1,5.0) {Frequency};
\node[below] at (3.5,-1.0) {Shoe size};
\node at (3.5,11.1) {Class shoe sizes};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
